explanation:
Here is a complete tiny JavaScript file:
```javascript
const message = 'Let us build something!';
console.log(message);
```
const gives a name to a value you do not reassign. The equals sign stores the text in message. Quote marks make a string, meaning text. console.log displays its argument; parentheses hold that argument. Running this file prints Let us build something! once. The semicolons finish the statements.

This complete JavaScript example is valid code with a logic mistake:
```javascript
function combine(a, b) {
  return a - b;
}
const actual = combine(8, 3);
const expected = 11;
console.log(actual);
console.log(actual === expected);
```
It prints 5 and false. The function receives inputs a and b; return sends back their difference. The code runs, but the intended operation was addition. === compares the actual value with the expected value; it does not assign a new value.

Replace the mistaken function with this complete corrected example:
```javascript
function combine(a, b) {
  return a + b;
}
console.log(combine(8, 3));
console.log(combine(0, 3));
console.log(combine(-4, 9));
console.log(combine(8, 3) === 11);
```
The results are 11, 3, 5, and true. + adds these numeric arguments. Zero changes nothing in addition; -4 plus 9 is 5. Checking several meaningful numeric cases helps tell whether the rule works beyond one example.

package.json is JSON, a structured text format. This complete example declares the start script:
```json
{
  "name": "launch-demo",
  "scripts": {
    "start": "node main.js"
  }
}
```
Double quotes surround JSON property names and text values. Curly braces hold objects, colons separate names from values, and commas separate properties. The nested scripts object maps a script name to its command. JSON does not permit comments or trailing commas.

The browser exercise asks you to represent a command sequence as JavaScript data. This separate example prints two commands but does not execute them:
```javascript
const commands = ['cd launch-demo', 'npm start'];
console.log(commands.join('\n'));
```
The output is two lines: cd launch-demo, then npm start. The brackets create an array; join combines its strings; `\n` is the newline character. console.log shows the text. It does not change the browser's folder or launch a terminal.

The browser exercise is a simplified version list, not actual Git. This complete JavaScript example reads its latest saved record:
```javascript
const versions = [
  { message: 'First greeting', code: 'Hi' },
  { message: 'Friendlier greeting', code: 'Hi, friend' }
];
function latestCopy(history) {
  return { ...history[history.length - 1] };
}
console.log(latestCopy(versions));
console.log(versions.length);
```
It returns the second record and then 2. Array indexes begin at 0, so length - 1 finds the last item. Object spread returns a new flat object without changing the history.

