explanation:
A health check is a small response about readiness. This complete standalone JavaScript example requires both the app and its storage to be ready:
```javascript
function readiness(appReady, storageReady, version) {
  return {
    status: appReady && storageReady ? 200 : 503,
    version
  };
}
console.log(readiness(true, true, 'demo-4'));
console.log(readiness(true, false, 'demo-4'));
```
The first result has status 200 and version demo-4; the second has 503 and the same version. && requires both conditions. Object shorthand version means version: version. Including the version on failure still identifies the release you are examining.

Here is a complete separate local Node server. Save as health-server.mjs and run it with Node:
```javascript
import { createServer } from 'node:http';
const version = process.env.RELEASE_ID || 'demo-4';
const appReady = true;
createServer((req, res) => {
  if (req.url !== '/health') {
    res.writeHead(404);
    res.end('Not found');
    return;
  }
  const storageReady = process.env.STORAGE_READY !== 'false';
  const status = appReady && storageReady ? 200 : 503;
  res.writeHead(status, { 'Content-Type': 'application/json' });
  res.end(JSON.stringify({ status, version }));
}).listen(3030, '127.0.0.1');
```
/health returns a JSON readiness result; other paths return 404. createServer registers the request callback; writeHead chooses status and headers; end finishes the response. Environment variables configure this learning flag and version.

The coding exercise combines three mandatory boolean review results. Here is a separate complete JavaScript example:
```javascript
function ready(review) {
  return review.permissions === true
    && review.secrets === true
    && review.keyboard === true;
}
console.log(ready({ permissions: true, secrets: true, keyboard: true }));
console.log(ready({ permissions: true, secrets: false, keyboard: true }));
console.log(ready({ permissions: 'true', secrets: true, keyboard: true }));
console.log(ready({ permissions: true }));
```
The outputs are true, false, false, false. === true requires an actual true boolean. A missing field is undefined and fails. && allows release only when all three comparisons pass.

The browser exercise below is only a model of selecting a branch. An object maps branch names to records.
```javascript
const saved = {main: {code: "Hello"}, draft: {code: "Hi"}};
console.log(saved["draft"].code);
console.log(saved["main"].code);
```

Object.hasOwn(branches, current) checks that the requested branch is an own entry of the map. If not, return null before reading its code.

```javascript
const branches = {main: "working code"};
console.log(Object.hasOwn(branches, "main"));
console.log(Object.hasOwn(branches, "draft"));
```
This prints true, then false. The function receives an object and property key and reports only its own properties, without modifying the map.

